able"],"gaps":["How many gallons per minute the well still delivers in July","Rootworm pressure on this field (traits and insecticide use)"],"risks":["Water right limits in dry years like 2011, 2012 and 2022","Falling well yield as the aquifer declines","Corn rootworm resistance after many corn years"],"ask":["How many more years can my well supply a full corn crop?","What changes is GMD 3 planning for irrigation limits?"]},"opt_cswg":{"title":"#3 Corn \u00b7 soybean \u00b7 wheat \u00b7 sorghum","why":["Spreads risk over four crops and two growing seasons. Every crop gets limited irrigation, so no single year needs more than your water right."],"evidence":["NASA POWER weather replay: within the water right in all 42 seasons","Previous-crop effects from extension literature (to be sourced by the scorer team)"],"benefits":["7.5 in less irrigation per year than continuous corn","Fertilizer N falls to 95 lb/acre","Four crops spread price and weather risk"],"gaps":["Yield of limited-irrigation corn and soybean on this field","Labor for four crops instead of one"],"risks":["Tight window from soybean harvest to wheat seeding in late September","Needs a drill and header for small grains","Four markets to manage"],"ask":["Which wheat variety fits late seeding after soybean?","How do limited-irrigation corn yields compare in K-State trials near Garden City?"]},"opt_sfw":{"title":"#2 Sorghum \u00b7 sunflower \u00b7 wheat","why":["Uses the least groundwater. Sorghum and sunflower root deep and handle heat; wheat grows in the cool season when evaporation is low."],"evidence":["NASA POWER weather replay: 4.2 in per year, within the water right in all 42 seasons","Sunflower and wheat are dryland here, so their income depends on rain (see the range)"],"benefits":["11.9 in less irrigation per year than continuous corn","No crop follows itself, so pest cycles break","Fertilizer N falls to 88 lb/acre"],"gaps":["Dryland sunflower and wheat yields on this field","A sunflower buyer within trucking distance"],"risks":["The fallow after wheat leaves soil bare in the windy spring","Sunflower dries the subsoil before wheat","Lower and more variable income than corn"],"ask":["Can I sell sunflower locally, and at what discount?","Would my lender accept lower income in exchange for water savings?"]},"opt_cst":{"title":"#1 Corn \u00b7 soybean + triticale cover","why":["The smallest change: corn stays every second year and your machinery fits. Soybean breaks the corn rootworm cycle and leaves nitrogen for the next corn. Triticale covers the soil over winter."],"evidence":["NASA POWER weather replay: 3.5 in less irrigation per year than continuous corn","FAO-56 crop coefficients for corn and soybean (values to be confirmed by the scorer)","Previous crop from CDL 2025: corn"],"benefits":["Bare soil drops from 6 to 4 months per year","Fertilizer N falls from 200 to 90 lb/acre","Same corn markets as today"],"gaps":["How much spring soil water triticale takes on this silt loam","Soybean yield under limited irrigation in Kearny County"],"risks":["Fits the water right in only 25 of 42 seasons: corn after a cover crop needs more water in dry springs","Triticale must be terminated on time or it competes with corn","Distance to a soybean buyer"],"ask":["When must I terminate triticale before corn to protect soil water?","Does crop insurance cover corn after a cover crop?"]}},"constraints":[{"ok":true,"label":"Irrigation available","note":"center pivot","id":"irrigation"},{"ok":true,"label":"Water right 18 in/yr","note":"assumed","id":"water_right"},{"ok":true,"label":"Small-grain equipment","note":"for wheat, triticale","id":"small_grain"},{"ok":false,"label":"Livestock on farm","note":"no forage need","id":"livestock"},{"ok":true,"label":"Start season 2027","note":"","id":"start"},{"ok":true,"label":"Previous crop: corn","note":"from USDA CDL","id":"previous_crop"}]}